WEBVTT

00:00:00.561 --> 00:00:24.241
<v Riley>Hey there — today's a heavy one. We're tracking expanding evacuation orders out of 70 Mile House, an out-of-control fire near Logan Lake, and a month-old blaze near Lillooet that just came roaring back fifteen times its size. Then in the deep dive: when every route out is jammed at once, what actually determines whether you get into — or out of — the backcountry? That and more, coming right up.

00:00:40.216 --> 00:01:02.144
<v Riley>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities — I'm Riley. It's Friday, July 31st, 2026. Coming to you from the Cariboo, on lands that have been cared for by the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh peoples since time immemorial. Today's theme is Wild Spaces and Outdoor Life.

00:01:03.144 --> 00:01:34.864
<v Casey>And I'm Casey. We're talking backcountry, wildfire, and everything that decides whether you actually get to enjoy either one this summer. Quick weather check — Horsefly Lake is sitting at 13 degrees with mainly clear skies, high of 24 today, and there's a thunderstorm forecast tomorrow with about 4 millimetres of rain. Around the region: 100 Mile House at 12 and partly cloudy, Williams Lake at 14 and overcast, Quesnel at 14 and partly cloudy, and out on the Chilcotin plateau, Riske Creek is sitting at 14 with overcast skies.

00:01:35.235 --> 00:01:39.243
<v Riley>Overcast for a reason today — a lot of that is smoke. Let's get into it.

00:01:40.643 --> 00:02:09.651
<v Riley>It's a heavy wildfire day across the feed — a dozen stories at least touching fire, evacuation, or the roads and power lines that connect to them — so we're spending real time there before we get to anything lighter. BC Hydro announced it's beginning restoration work and damage assessments in areas hit by the Big Bar and Brunswick Complex wildfires — crews are going in to check poles, lines, and substations before power gets fully restored to affected communities.

00:02:10.899 --> 00:02:16.195
<v Casey>That's the unglamorous part nobody photographs — walking every pole before you flip the switch back on.

00:02:17.095 --> 00:02:35.431
<v Casey>The same wildfire complex is behind an expanding evacuation order — 70 Mile House was ordered out yesterday morning, with the Thompson-Nicola Regional District citing projected growth of the Pear Lake wildfire toward the community. That's a community of a few thousand people with essentially one way in and one way out.

00:02:36.746 --> 00:02:48.250
<v Riley>BC Wildfire Service and Logan Lake crews spent the overnight hours fighting a fire that's still out of control at 88 hectares, even after earlier containment efforts looked like they'd held.

00:02:48.750 --> 00:03:13.238
<v Casey>North of here, the same pattern shows up with a sharper edge. Ricochet is reporting that in northern Ontario, members of some First Nations are challenging evacuation checkpoints directly — using speedboats to get around blockades because they say elders were left behind when fires closed in from the north and west. Gull Bay First Nation's chief has been calling for all members to leave as fires approach from two directions at once.

00:03:14.657 --> 00:03:23.657
<v Riley>That's what happens when an evacuation order doesn't account for who's actually still on the land, or who gets left off the list when the buses fill up.

00:03:25.205 --> 00:03:50.613
<v Riley>Europe is dealing with its own version at a much larger scale — Wildfire Today reports synchronized fires across multiple countries are testing a newly coordinated European response system, with hundreds of thousands of people displaced and firefighters battling walls of flame in several regions simultaneously. Officials there are explicitly framing it as: no country should have to face this scale of fire alone.

00:03:51.722 --> 00:03:56.754
<v Casey>Same lesson, bigger map — a response system only works if it's built for more than one fire at a time.

00:03:58.164 --> 00:04:12.668
<v Casey>There's also a grimmer entry in that block. Wildfire Today is reporting a fourth wildland firefighter has died from an entrapment incident on the Knowles Fire near the Colorado-Utah border. Crews there are mourning multiple losses from the same fire this season.

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:20.496
<v Riley>That one's a reminder that behind every containment number is a crew of people standing between the fire and the highway.

00:04:22.418 --> 00:04:37.282
<v Riley>On the science side, there's an interesting companion piece to all this. Researchers used AI to analyze songbird evolution and found it happens in short explosive bursts tied to major climate shifts, rather than a slow, steady drift over millions of years.

00:04:37.182 --> 00:04:42.230
<v Casey>Bursts, then long quiet stretches — sounds a lot like this fire season compared to last one.

00:04:43.546 --> 00:05:02.578
<v Casey>And sticking with the evolutionary angle for a moment, Nautilus has a piece asking whether early humans preferentially hunted female mammoths — new isotope work on kill sites suggests herd structure may have made females more accessible targets, which matters for understanding how quickly megafauna populations could collapse under sustained pressure.

00:05:03.987 --> 00:05:18.555
<v Riley>Rounding out that arc, the New York Times reports there's roughly a ton of space debris falling to Earth every week now, unpredictably, with flaming chunks landing on homes, farms, and parking lots — and nobody's settled who's actually liable when it happens.

00:05:19.055 --> 00:05:30.079
<v Casey>Less "wild spaces" and more "wild airspace," but the liability question rhymes with a lot of what we talk about here — who's accountable when something falls onto land nobody consented to have it fall on.

00:05:31.732 --> 00:05:49.716
<v Casey>Pivoting closer to home, there's some genuinely good wildfire news for once. A significant, coordinated 24-hour response involving over a hundred personnel — BC Wildfire Service crews, local contract crews, heavy equipment operators, local fire departments, and aviation resources — kept growth on the Felker fire to a minimum.

00:05:51.028 --> 00:05:57.324
<v Riley>That's the response actually catching up to the fire, which doesn't happen nearly often enough this summer.

00:05:58.741 --> 00:06:31.189
<v Riley>The 100 Mile Legion Branch 260 and the local Lions Club also gave a combined eleven-thousand-dollar contribution to the South Cariboo Health Foundation, kicking off their annual fundraising campaign with a strong start. And over in the Kootenays, The Tyee has a feature on the Nupqu Native Plant Nursery, run through Ktunaxa territory, which distributes about 700,000 seedlings a year to land reclamation projects across their traditional territory — Indigenous-led ecological restoration at a genuinely large scale.

00:06:32.622 --> 00:06:39.350
<v Casey>That's the kind of infrastructure story that doesn't get headlines but quietly rebuilds a landscape one seedling at a time.

00:06:39.850 --> 00:07:03.770
<v Casey>On the tech side of things, Boing Boing and Tom's Hardware are both reporting that AI companies have been quietly buying up physical books through book databases like ISBNdb, scanning them, and destroying the originals to train language models — a legally protected practice under fair use rulings, but an uncomfortable one, especially given that our own scripts get written with tools from one of the companies named in that reporting.

00:07:04.926 --> 00:07:22.942
<v Riley>And in a slightly friendlier AI story, OpenAI announced it's giving free ChatGPT access to 100,000 scientists, mathematicians, and engineers at academic institutions through a new Academic Researchers program — lowering the barrier to frontier tools for people who'd otherwise be priced out.

00:07:24.535 --> 00:07:39.991
<v Riley>Also today, Krebs on Security has a warning about generic TV streaming sticks that promise unlimited content for a one-time fee — security researchers say a lot of these secretly rent out your home internet connection to strangers as a residential proxy network.

00:07:41.404 --> 00:07:46.644
<v Casey>Worth checking what's plugged into your router before your bandwidth becomes someone else's business model.

00:07:47.981 --> 00:08:00.133
<v Casey>And ABC has formally filed opposition with the FCC over what it's calling a punitive early license renewal push on its broadcast stations, arguing the move is designed to chill news coverage the commission doesn't like.

00:08:00.771 --> 00:08:05.355
<v Riley>That one matters for anyone who cares who holds a broadcast license and why.

00:08:06.014 --> 00:08:08.598
<v Riley>That's the roundup. Let's head into the spotlight.

00:08:09.998 --> 00:08:38.094
<v Casey>Scout Island Nature Centre in Williams Lake is running kids' summer programs all season, put on by the Williams Lake Field Naturalists — volunteer-run, no big budget, just a marsh boardwalk and people who know the birds by call. Each week has its own theme, Water one week, Bugs the next, Birds after that, and there's Salmon in the Classroom programming tied in for the fall. It's the kind of low-key, hands-on outdoor education that doesn't need an app or a subscription. Registration's at scoutisland.ca.

00:08:38.720 --> 00:08:46.712
<v Riley>(warmly) Volunteer-run nature programming that's outlasted a dozen flashier initiatives — that kind of thing earns support on its own merits.

00:08:47.412 --> 00:08:52.308
<v Casey>Speaking of getting out into wild places — that's exactly where today's deep dive is headed.

00:08:53.708 --> 00:09:01.812
<v Riley>When a wildfire season stacks up multiple simultaneous closures, what actually determines whether people can get into — or out of — wild places at all?

00:09:02.155 --> 00:09:12.003
<v Casey>Because the honest answer isn't about permits or promotion campaigns. It's about how many roads a place has, and what happens when all of them are competing for the same traffic at once.

00:09:12.521 --> 00:09:30.129
<v Riley>Highway 24 is the case study sitting right in front of us. BC Highway Patrol just ran a safety surge there — unmarked SUVs, motorcycles, the full commercial vehicle safety enforcement setup — because wildfire closures elsewhere are pushing more traffic onto that corridor than it's built to absorb.

00:09:30.529 --> 00:09:52.689
<v Casey>And it's not a one-off corridor problem. Highway 3 closed east of Hope this summer. Highway 1 closed at multiple points along its length. A fire near Lytton shut down a long stretch of the Trans-Canada and rerouted freight traffic hundreds of kilometres out of its way. The Fraser Canyon had both major routes closed at once, with evacuation orders expanding near Boston Bar at the same time.

00:09:53.363 --> 00:10:04.003
<v Riley>So Highway 24 isn't an isolated bottleneck — it's absorbing overflow from a province-wide corridor squeeze hitting almost every major route through the Interior this season.

00:10:04.569 --> 00:10:27.689
<v Casey>Right. And the Riley Creek fire near Lillooet is a good illustration of why "held" doesn't mean "over." It sparked June 24th, grew to 72 hectares, and BC Wildfire Service declared it held on July 15th. As of Thursday evening it's mapped at 1,150 hectares and spreading toward the Thompson-Nicola Regional District, with the Squamish-Lillooet Regional District expanding its alerts.

00:10:28.189 --> 00:10:47.445
<v Riley>That's a technology story too, even though it doesn't look like one at first glance. A month-old fire re-igniting and spreading fifteenfold isn't a surprise if you're watching satellite thermal imaging and BC Wildfire's public hotspot layers closely — the tools to see this kind of flare-up coming exist and are publicly accessible.

00:10:47.968 --> 00:11:00.240
<v Casey>(deadpan) Seeing it coming and having a second place to route traffic to are two completely different problems. DriveBC can tell you Highway 1 is closed. It can't conjure a third route through the Fraser Canyon that doesn't already exist.

00:11:00.755 --> 00:11:30.419
<v Riley>No, but better real-time routing and predictive closure modeling changes outcomes even without laying new pavement. If Highway 24 is getting hit with an enforcement surge because volume spiked faster than anyone expected, that's as much a data and forecasting gap as a concrete one — commercial vehicle inspectors can't physically be everywhere, but a model that flags where surge traffic is headed a day or two out lets them position ahead of it instead of reacting after the fact.

00:11:31.041 --> 00:11:45.529
<v Casey>That helps a patrol plan its shift. It doesn't help a family in 70 Mile House who just got an evacuation order with one road out — and that road might be the same one everyone else in the region is using to flee the same fire complex at the same time.

00:11:46.129 --> 00:12:09.993
<v Riley>That's actually the same access question recreation users face, just with lower stakes on a good day. A backcountry traveler heading onto the Chilcotin plateau right now is making a decision built on the same single-corridor exposure. If the roads past Riske Creek get cut by smoke or closure, there frequently isn't a second way out — you wait it out or you turn around days before you planned to.

00:12:10.561 --> 00:12:26.153
<v Casey>Which means "getting out into wild spaces" for recreation and "getting people out of wild spaces" for emergency evacuation are the same underlying infrastructure question, and this region has never had redundancy built into that. It's single-threaded, and it has been for decades.

00:12:26.686 --> 00:12:56.534
<v Riley>There's a different failure mode worth setting alongside this — same underlying question of who controls access to public land under pressure. The Bureau of Land Management approved an 86-acre AI data center near Lake Mead on land that had already gone through review and been approved for a solar energy project — and made the switch without a new environmental review or public comment period, under a federal executive order fast-tracking data center buildout on public land.

00:12:57.073 --> 00:13:19.481
<v Casey>That's not adaptive reuse, that's a bait-and-switch on an approval process. The land cleared review once, for a specific use, and the substitution to something with a completely different footprint — 167 megawatts of peak demand, a dedicated 230-kilovolt line into the Mead substation, backup diesel generators — skipped every accountability step that made the original approval legitimate.

00:13:20.139 --> 00:13:30.427
<v Riley>It's already disturbed land next to an existing transmission corridor, though. That's arguably less damaging than siting the same facility somewhere untouched and greenfield.

00:13:31.078 --> 00:13:54.310
<v Casey>(wry) "Arguably less damaging" is exactly the argument that gets used to justify skipping review every time this kind of swap happens. Lake Mead sits ten miles away, already at historic lows after years of Colorado River drought. Adding a facility with that kind of power draw and diesel backup nearby isn't a footnote — it's added pressure on a fragile system with zero public input on whether that tradeoff was even acceptable.

00:13:54.810 --> 00:14:10.826
<v Riley>The process failure is the actual problem there, not the siting itself. If they'd run a fresh review and it still landed on that parcel, that's a defensible outcome. Skipping the review entirely because an executive order said to skip it is a different and worse thing.

00:14:11.448 --> 00:14:31.880
<v Casey>Right — and that connects to what we're actually talking about today. It's not really about data centers or highways specifically. It's about whether the public gets a say before access to a landscape changes, whether that's a road getting overwhelmed by diverted wildfire traffic or a drought-stressed watershed getting a new industrial neighbor with no comment period at all.

00:14:32.432 --> 00:14:54.104
<v Riley>So what does that look like translated to the Cariboo specifically? Not a public comment period on every highway closure — those genuinely are emergency decisions that can't wait on process. But corridor redundancy planning is a decision that could involve public input years before the next fire season, and right now nobody's forcing that conversation to happen.

00:14:54.657 --> 00:15:15.033
<v Casey>Who would even hold that conversation, though? The Ministry of Transportation plans highway capacity on its own multi-year cycle, driven by traffic counts and budget allocations that have nothing to do with wildfire risk modeling. The Cariboo Regional District and TNRD don't set provincial highway budgets, and they don't control what gets prioritized in Victoria.

00:15:15.626 --> 00:15:50.730
<v Riley>They can still put pressure on it, though. Regional districts submit resolutions to the Union of BC Municipalities every year, and those resolutions do shape provincial priorities over successive budget cycles — not overnight. A resolution specifically asking the province to formally assess single-corridor risk for communities like 100 Mile House, 70 Mile House, and the Chilcotin plateau communities, given how many recent fire seasons have produced overlapping closures, would put an actual number on how exposed this region is instead of leaving it as anecdote.

00:15:51.370 --> 00:16:10.026
<v Casey>That's a real lever, and cheaper than waiting for the next Highway 24 traffic surge to make headlines again next August. It still doesn't fix this season's problem, though. The road you've got is the road you've got until that assessment turns into an actual second route, and that takes years even after a resolution passes.

00:16:10.426 --> 00:16:27.882
<v Riley>No argument there. But naming the structural gap now, while the Riley Creek fire and the Big Bar complex are both actively proving the point in real time, is a far better moment to start that resolution than waiting for a quiet, smoke-free winter when nobody remembers why the exposure mattered.

00:16:28.569 --> 00:16:56.481
<v Casey>The one thing actually worth doing is getting the exposure documented while the evidence is this fresh, so it's not just a story people half-remember by the time someone finally asks the province to fund a second way out. Williams Lake and Quesnel both sit at junctions with more route options than 100 Mile House or the plateau communities do. The places with the least redundancy right now are exactly the ones least likely to get attention unless someone puts the data in front of the province deliberately.

00:16:57.109 --> 00:17:09.917
<v Riley>And that's a concrete, low-cost first step any regional district here could actually take this fall — not a grant application, not a pilot project, just a documented ask backed by this season's own closure record.

00:17:10.239 --> 00:17:13.463
<v Casey>(chuckles) If it actually gets submitted instead of talked about, sure.

00:17:13.996 --> 00:17:26.916
<v Riley>Fair enough — and if any of you listening know whether your own regional district has ever put something like that forward, that's exactly the kind of tip worth sending our way. Email us at feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a.

00:17:27.502 --> 00:17:35.398
<v Casey>And if you're driving anywhere near Highway 24 this weekend, give yourself the extra ten minutes — it's not going to be empty. Have a good one out there.

00:17:35.734 --> 00:17:39.366
<v Riley>(warmly) Stay safe, stay smoke-aware, and we'll catch you next episode.